---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "almanac/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptCast from "almanac/components/PromptCast.astro";
import { ExecCache, execute } from "almanac/exec";
import { THEMES } from "almanac/themes";
import { withBase } from "almanac/paths";

const docs = await getCollection("docs");
const posts = await getCollection("blog");

// Run for real, through the same engine that powers `exec` blocks in Markdown,
// so the number below is computed while this page is built rather than typed.
const DEMO = `const sunrise = (dayOfYear, latitude) => {
	const decl = -23.44 * Math.cos(((2 * Math.PI) / 365) * (dayOfYear + 10));
	const rad = Math.PI / 180;
	const cosH = -Math.tan(latitude * rad) * Math.tan(decl * rad);
	return 12 - Math.acos(cosH) / rad / 15;
};

console.log(sunrise(180, 51.48).toFixed(2), "hours after midnight, London");`;

const cache = new ExecCache(`${process.cwd()}/.almanac/exec`);
const demo = await execute({ code: DEMO, language: "js" }, cache);

const structuredData = {
	"@context": "https://schema.org",
	"@type": "SoftwareSourceCode",
	name: "Almanac",
	description:
		"A publishing framework for technical and scientific writing: docs, blog, and executable content from one source.",
	codeRepository: "https://github.com/shravanngoswamii/almanac",
	programmingLanguage: "TypeScript",
	author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Shravan Goswami" },
};
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Almanac"
	description="A publishing framework for technical and scientific writing. Docs, blog, and executable content from one source."
	structuredData={structuredData}
>
	<section class="hero">
		<h1>Documentation that runs its own examples.</h1>
		<div class="actions">
			<a class="button" href={withBase('/docs/start/installation/')}>Get started</a>
			<a class="button secondary" href={withBase('/docs/')}>Read the docs</a>
		</div>
		<div class="hero-demo">
			<div class="terminal-chrome">
				<span class="chrome-dots">
					<span></span><span></span><span></span>
				</span>
				<span class="chrome-title">almanac · quick start</span>
			</div>
			<PromptCast />
		</div>
		<p class="hero-copy">
			<code>Almanac</code> is a publishing framework for technical and scientific
			writing. It gives you documentation, a blog, search, and {THEMES.length} themes
			from one directory of Markdown, and it can run your code during the build so
			the output on the page is the real thing.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-header">
			<h2>Your examples cannot go stale.</h2>
			<p>
				Mark a fenced block with <code>exec</code> and Almanac runs it while the
				site builds. The output below was computed a moment ago, not pasted in.
			</p>
		</div>
		<div class="exec-showcase">
			<Code
				code={DEMO}
				lang="js"
				themes={{ light: "github-light", dark: "github-dark" }}
				defaultColor={false}
			/>
			<figure class="exec-result" data-exec-state="ok">
				<figcaption class="exec-caption">
					<span>Output</span>
					<span class="exec-meta">{demo.cached ? 'cached' : `${demo.durationMs}ms`}</span>
				</figcaption>
				<pre class="exec-stream exec-stdout"><code>{demo.stdout}</code></pre>
			</figure>
		</div>
		<p class="exec-showcase-note">
			An almanac has always been a book of computed tables. This one computes them
			at build time, keys the cache on the code, the language, and the runtime
			version, and never runs an unchanged block twice.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-header">
			<h2>Clone it and run it.</h2>
			<p>Not on npm yet. The starter in the repository is a complete site, and this one is built from the same code.</p>
		</div>
		<div class="install-strip">
			<div>
				<strong>Run the starter</strong>
				<span>You own two things: the config file and your content. The framework handles the rest.</span>
			</div>
			<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/shravanngoswamii/almanac.git
cd almanac &amp;&amp; pnpm install
pnpm --filter starter dev</code></pre>
			<a class="button secondary" href={withBase('/docs/start/installation/')}>Installation guide</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-header">
			<h2>Documentation.</h2>
			<p>{docs.length} pages covering configuration, writing, theming, search, and deployment.</p>
		</div>
		<div class="docs-grid">
			<a class="doc-link" href={withBase('/docs/start/quick-start/')}>
				<h3>Quick start</h3>
				<p>Go from an empty directory to a running documentation site.</p>
			</a>
			<a class="doc-link" href={withBase('/docs/guides/executable-code/')}>
				<h3>Executable code</h3>
				<p>Run JavaScript and TypeScript blocks at build time and embed their output.</p>
			</a>
			<a class="doc-link" href={withBase('/docs/reference/configuration/')}>
				<h3>Configuration</h3>
				<p>Every option, its default, and what is still on the roadmap.</p>
			</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section attribution-section">
		<p class="attribution">
			Almanac grew out of <a href="https://github.com/shravanngoswamii/flect" target="_blank">Flect</a>,
			an Astro documentation template by the same author, and inherited its design
			system. <a href={withBase('/blog/from-template-to-framework/')}>That story is on the blog</a>,
			along with {posts.length - 1} other posts.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-header">
			<h2>FAQ</h2>
			<p>Including the parts that are not built yet.</p>
		</div>
		<div class="faq-list">
			<details class="faq-item">
				<summary>What is Almanac?
					<svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
						<path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
					</svg>
				</summary>
				<p>An Astro integration that turns a directory of Markdown into a documentation site with a sidebar, search, a blog, {THEMES.length} themes, and code blocks that execute during the build.</p>
			</details>
			<details class="faq-item">
				<summary>Which languages can it execute?
					<svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
						<path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
					</svg>
				</summary>
				<p>JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, and R, each in its own process with a timeout. Python runs through Pyodide and R through WebR, so neither needs a system installation. Naming a kernel with <code>kernel=python3</code> sends a block to a Jupyter server instead, for environments Almanac cannot install itself, and its images and tables render. A language without a runner renders as an ordinary highlighted block rather than an error.</p>
			</details>
			<details class="faq-item">
				<summary>Can it produce a PDF?
					<svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
						<path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
					</svg>
				</summary>
				<p>Yes, and this site does it. Every documentation page here has a PDF link at the bottom, typeset by Typst from the same document tree the web page came from, executed output included. No system Typst needed. What is missing is templates: the styling is not yet configurable.</p>
			</details>
			<details class="faq-item">
				<summary>How does it compare to Starlight or Docusaurus?
					<svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
						<path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
					</svg>
				</summary>
				<p>Both are far more mature and better supported, and neither executes code or typesets a PDF from the same source. If you want a docs site backed by a team, use them. Almanac exists for technical and scientific writing where the output of your code belongs on the page.</p>
			</details>
			<details class="faq-item">
				<summary>Is it ready for my project?
					<svg class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
						<path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
					</svg>
				</summary>
				<p>It builds this site, so it works. It is also not on npm yet, pre-1.0, and maintained by one person. Clone the repository and run the starter if you want to try it. Treat it as early software that is honest about being early.</p>
			</details>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
